From f2f1957657529c925e8e78443526be4eacccab03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: logdog2325 <loganjayfisher@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 11:20:04 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Netplay: let a joiner submit their own team from the room

Until now the host had to build BOTH players' teams before hosting,
because netplay ships the host's GBA saves to everyone at start and the
joiner's local team files are never used. Now a joiner pastes their own
Showdown export (or a pokepast.es link) in the room and the host writes
it into the socket-3 save it is about to sync.

Wire: a new TeamData message alongside ChatMessage, carrying the export
as text -- not a prebuilt party, because the Pokemon must be built
against the HOST save's trainer name and ID. A pokepaste link is
resolved on the submitting device, so the host only ever parses text it
was handed and never fetches a URL a stranger chose. The payload is
capped, and the parser it feeds is name-whitelisted, bounded, and writes
through the verified writer with a .bak, so a hostile paste can at worst
produce a silly-but-legal party.

The write is the shared UICommon injector (Core cannot call uicommon, so
it hops through a NetPlayUI virtual that both the Qt dialog and the
Android JNI callbacks implement) and completes BEFORE the ack, so the
file is on disk before any Start can read it. Result goes to the room
chat -- "<player> submitted a team: 6 Pokemon written to the guest save"
-- so both sides see it land. Any party size is accepted; people play
whatever format they like. A submission during a running battle is
rejected honestly: saves are already synced and the live core owns the
file, so it would be silently discarded.

Buttons on all three platforms: a Submit Team button in the desktop
room's chat panel, and on Android the joiner's FAB where the host's
Start button sits (only one of them is ever visible). Any host can
accept any joiner's team -- the injector is in the layer Android,
macOS and Windows all compile.
